 ## Window Semantics
 
-On the PostgreSQL reader a session is in a date window if and only if `session_dims.started_at` is in `[start_dt, end_dt]` (inclusive, bounds truncated to ms). The predicate lives once, in `_sessions_cte` (`sel`); every windowed fact query scopes or inner-joins to `sel` and carries no day, timestamp or hour predicate, so an in-window session contributes all its data whatever the fact's own day, and a session that started before the window contributes nothing. Sessions without `started_at` are excluded. Session-detail and skill-name lookups keyed by `session_id` are not windowed.
+On the PostgreSQL reader a session is in a date window (EPMCDME-16496) if `session_dims.started_at` is in `[start_dt, end_dt]` (inclusive, bounds truncated to ms) AND the session has ever produced at least one `turns_daily`, `tool_facts_daily`, `cost_daily`, `session_usage` or `lines_daily` row (`_has_any_activity`, no day bound), OR it has one of those same five rows on a day inside `[start_dt, end_dt]` (`_active_in_window`, day-bound). The predicate lives once, in `_sessions_cte` (`sel`); every windowed fact query scopes or inner-joins to `sel` and carries no day, timestamp or hour predicate of its own, so an in-window session contributes all its data whatever the fact's own day, and a session that is out of the window on both halves of the predicate contributes nothing. Sessions without `started_at` are excluded. Session-detail and skill-name lookups keyed by `session_id` are not windowed.
+
+This is a hybrid, not a pure activity check: a session whose `started_at` falls inside `[start_dt, end_dt]` keeps the exact sub-day window-boundary precision earlier design decisions (EPMCDME-15506/15584) depend on, as long as it has produced at least one real signal, ever — `_has_any_activity` gates that arm so a session that only ever got `session.start`/`session.env` hooks (e.g. a VS Code restart reopening a panel, a Copilot session closed without a prompt) is excluded from `sel` entirely, not merely flagged. The second half (`_active_in_window`, day-bound EXISTS checks against the window's day bounds) only *additionally* rescues a session whose `started_at` is outside the window but has fresh activity in it — an old session resumed with no new activity in the window stays excluded, one resumed with fresh activity is included. A day-grain rollup table has no time-of-day column, so this activity check cannot distinguish "earlier today, outside a narrow hour-scale window" from "inside it": a resumed session is rescued for the whole day its fresh activity falls on, not just the narrower hour/minute range a filter may ask for. **Known residual gap:** this day-grain imprecision is deliberately left as-is (ticket EPMCDME-16496 decision) — closing it would mean reading raw, non-rollup event tables (`hook_events`/`log_events`/`spans`) for admission instead of the day-partitioned rollups, which exist specifically to avoid that scan cost.
+
+Evaluate `_active_in_window(alias)` / `_has_any_activity(alias)` only once per query, against a single table alias, never twice against the same alias within one derived-table SELECT list (e.g. once in that subquery's own WHERE clause and again in its own SELECT list): under live PostgreSQL this duplication silently drops matching rows against these day-partitioned tables, rather than raising an error.
+
+**Out of scope:** Copilot subagent fragments appearing as separate sessions (ticket gap (c)) is not reproducible against this schema (no cross-session linkage; `subagent_invocations`' primary key is session-scoped) and is not addressed here; see EPMCDME-16572 if it is ever substantiated against this repo.
 
 The window decides membership only. The start, end and duration a session reports are its activity range (see Notes for a reader), which can begin before `started_at`: a session whose first OTel event precedes its first hook is still in the window of its `started_at`.
 
-The section 6.6 latency budget (825 ms per endpoint) is unverified for these whole-session scans: no 30-day, ~28.5K-session run was available locally.
+Overview's `total_sessions` (`get_cost_kpis`), per-developer `session_count`/`session_ids` (`get_users`) and per-developer-per-day activity (`get_users_daily_activity`) all count every session of `sel`, priced or not — the same costless-inclusive universe the Sessions list already uses via `get_session_cost_facts(..., include_costless=True)`. This replaces the earlier "D1 rule" that restricted all three to the `cost_daily`-only (priced) universe. `get_users_daily_activity`'s day bucket comes from a `UNION` across all five activity tables (not just `cost_daily`), so a costless session's day comes from whichever table it actually has a row in. `get_class_lists(f, "all")` (the subscription-type/project-origin breakdown merged into Overview's and Cost's KPIs) likewise covers every session of `sel`, not only ones with a `cost_daily` row.
+
+`get_cost_kpis`'s `spend_sessions`/`total_cost_usd` and `get_users`'s `cost_usd` still require an actual `cost_daily` row on top of the personal-billed exclusion (`_spend(alias)`): `_spend` only encodes the billing-class check, so every caller must evaluate it against an alias that is NULL for a costless row (the `c` side of the `sel`/`cost_daily` join), never against `sel`'s own row, or a costless company-billed session is wrongly counted as a spend session.
+
+Every query that names a developer (`get_users`, `get_cost_by_user`, `get_users_daily_activity`, `get_session_cost_facts`, `get_users_last_active`) resolves it through the single `_DEVELOPER` constant: the session's own email, else its smallest non-empty `cost_daily` email, else its smallest non-empty `lines_daily` email, else `'unknown'`. The expression is self-contained (its own correlated subqueries against `s.session_id`), not borrowed from whatever `c`/`d` alias happens to be in scope, specifically so every one of those callers agrees on a costless session's developer the same way instead of silently diverging per query.
+
+The admission predicate now evaluates ten correlated EXISTS subqueries per `session_dims` row (`_has_any_activity` and `_active_in_window` each check five tables) instead of the original five. The section 6.6 latency budget (825 ms per endpoint) is unverified for these whole-session scans either way: no 30-day, ~28.5K-session run was available locally, before or after this ticket's work.
 
