+## 1. Original Context
+
+Jira ticket EPMCDME-16496 (Bug, labels Horizon1/codemie-analytics, status In Progress): "[CLI Analytics] Sessions without token usage are missing from CLI Analytics, so GitHub Copilot Chat sessions never appear."
+
+Full ticket description: CLI Analytics builds the Sessions list, session detail, and session count only from cost rows (cost_daily): reader.py get_session_cost_facts (Sessions list), get_session_detail_meta (detail, "Session not found" when the session has no cost row), Overview total_sessions. A session that has hook events but no agent.usage.request is stored and its tools are counted in Tools & Models, but it never appears as a session. Copilot Chat sends no token usage by design, so no Copilot Chat session can appear on the dashboards. A Chat session that runs on the Copilot CLI runtime in VS Code (non-Local) gets usage only from session.shutdown, which VS Code did not write even after the chat was closed, so it is missing too.
+
+Expected result: A session with hook events is listed in CLI Analytics (Sessions, session detail, Overview count) even without token usage, with its prompts, turns and tool calls and empty/"n/a" tokens and cost. Copilot Chat usage appears on the dashboards as the story requires.
+
+Comment 1 (Klim Starykau, 2026-10-29, verification FAILED against BE MR !4659 head 213371f22): the original case now passes (VS Code Copilot Chat Local session without token usage is listed, detail opens). But two regressions appeared: (a) every stored session is now listed including completely empty sessions (only agent.session.start + agent.session.env, e.g. from VS Code restarts reopening Claude Code panels, or Copilot desktop-app helper sessions, or Copilot sessions closed without a prompt) — these should not be listed, or at least counted as dead_sessions not regular sessions; (b) Copilot subagent fragments (each subagent runs under its own session id; the adapter forwards the subagent's prompt/tool hooks under that id) are now listed as separate sessions — they should not be, they belong to the parent (related ticket EPMCDME-16572).
+
+Comment 2 (Klim Starykau, 2026-11-02, re-verification FAILED against BE MR !4659 head cccf812a3, includes "Filter unlisted sessions in CLI analytics sessions CTE" and "Flag sub-agent sessions in session_dims"): the original case, the empty-Copilot-session case, and the subagent-fragment case are now FIXED. Remaining failure: a session with NO activity in the selected period is still listed IF it had activity before the period. Repro: `claude --resume`, pick an old session (last activity 2026-10-30), `/exit` without typing anything; this resumed session sends agent.session.start, agent.session.env, agent.session.end, agent.git.snapshot in the new period but no prompt/API call/tool call and no cost rows in that period. It is listed in the Sessions list and counted in Overview total_sessions for "Last hour", with turns=0, api_calls=0, tool_calls=0, tokens=0, and the prompt column showing the repository name instead of a real prompt. Before the backend change, such sessions were NOT listed, because listing used to start from cost rows in the period. Root cause as stated by the reporter: "The listing condition uses the session's all-time counters (session_dims.first_prompt / turns / api_calls / tool_calls in _IS_LISTED, reader.py), while the row's numbers are computed for the selected period. A session from an earlier day passes the condition and is shown with zeros whenever any event of it falls in the period." Also reproduces for plain Claude Code (not just Copilot): 6+ old sessions in "Last 7 days" are listed with 0 turns/api_calls/tool_calls because only session.end/git.snapshot fell in that window.
+
+Expected (per comment 2): "A session is listed and counted for a period only when it has activity (a prompt, an API call or a tool call) or cost rows in that period; otherwise it is not listed, or at least it is counted as a dead session, not as a regular one."
