+## 6. Risk Indicators
+
+- **Ticket context is ahead of this repo's code.** Both ticket comments were verified against BE MR !4659 (heads 213371f22, cccf812a3), which introduces `_IS_LISTED` and an `is_subagent` session_dims column neither of which exists anywhere in this repo's history. Implementation work here must be planned fresh against the current `a80c1922c`-based state, not assumed to be "the same bug, slightly regressed" from the ticket's narrative.
+- **Gap (a) — Overview/per-user/per-day counts are cost_daily-based while Sessions list is costless-inclusive** — confirmed real and current, and is a *documented, deliberate* design choice (the "D1 rule", handler.py:425-426, 607). Fixing it means explicitly revisiting/removing that documented rule, not just patching a bug — a product/design decision point, not purely mechanical.
+- **Gap (b) — empty sessions (only `agent.session.start` + `agent.session.env`) are listed as regular sessions** — confirmed real and current; `_dead_sessions` skips `cost <= 0` sessions entirely (`continue`), so zero-cost/zero-activity sessions are neither flagged dead nor excluded anywhere.
+- **Gap (c) — Copilot subagent fragments listed as separate sessions — NOT substantiated against current code/schema.** No table/column/query anywhere in `reader.py`, `rollups.py`, `fork_attribution.py`, or the 3 migrations supports a subagent emitting a session_id distinct from its parent; `subagent_invocations` PK is `(session_id, agent_id)` with agent_id always scoped within one session. If this symptom is real in production, either (i) some CLI client emits a genuinely different session_id per subagent run via a path not visible from the backend's storage/read side (would need separate confirmation from a CLI-side investigation, out of scope for this repo), or (ii) the symptom was specific to the as-yet-unmerged MR !4659 code and does not apply to this checkout. Do not plan a fix for this gap without a concrete repro against this repo's current ingest path.
+- **Gap (d) — membership keyed on all-time `session_dims.started_at` rather than period-scoped activity** — confirmed real and current, but its practical failure mode in THIS codebase is the mirror image of the ticket's framing: it wrongly **excludes** a still-active older session whose `started_at` falls outside the window (rather than wrongly including one), and it fails to clip a long session's fact rows (cost/lines/turns) to the window once admitted. The literal "old resumed session shown with zeros in the new period" scenario from Comment 2 requires the `_IS_LISTED`/all-time-counter logic that exists only in MR !4659, not in this checkout — re-derive the fix against this repo's actual `_sessions_cte`/`_scope` predicates rather than assuming the ticket's exact mechanism applies here.
+- `reader.py` is 1331 lines, already well past the ~500-line soft-threshold guideline for adding non-core concerns — any of the three still-open gaps risks accreting onto an already-oversized module; plan an extraction (new module for session-classification/listing predicates) alongside the fix per `.ai-run/guides/standards/code-quality.md` precedent (`workspace_script_bridge.py` → `job_bridge.py`/`tool_calling_limits.py`).
+- `make test-cli-analytics-live` is mandatory (non-skippable) for this change and requires Docker to spin up two throwaway Postgres containers — must be run and its real (non-skipped) output reported before any MR.
+- No git/doc history anywhere references EPMCDME-16496 or EPMCDME-16572 in this repo — no architectural precedent trail beyond the single `a80c1922c` precursor commit.
+- Independent, non-code-logic root cause to rule out separately: project-gate silent discards (`applications.cli_analytics_enabled=false` or a project-header mismatch returns HTTP 200 with nothing written) can produce "missing sessions" symptoms unrelated to the listing/classification logic analyzed here.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Summary for Complexity Assessment
+
+This task touches three layers: the repository/query layer (`reader.py`, 1331 lines, all session-universe and fact-scoping logic concentrated in `_sessions_cte`/`_scope`/`get_cost_kpis`/`get_users`/`get_users_daily_activity`), the service/handler layer (`cli_analytics_handler.py`, the documented "D1 rule" cost-based Overview universe and the `_dead_sessions` aggregate classifier), and the test layer (mandatory live-Postgres suite via `make test-cli-analytics-live`, plus the ~1900-line `test_cli_analytics_handler.py`). No DB migration is needed for two of the three confirmed-real remaining gaps: (a) Overview/per-user/per-day counts being cost_daily-based instead of matching the Sessions list's costless-inclusive universe is a query-only change in `get_cost_kpis`, `get_users`, `get_users_daily_activity` plus removal/revision of the handler's documented D1-rule comments; (b) empty sessions being listed as regular rather than dead is also query/handler-only, using session_dims' existing all-time `api_calls`/`tool_calls`/`turns` columns that `_sessions_cte` currently ignores. The fourth gap, period-scoped membership (d), is also fixable without migration using the already-existing day/hour-grain rollup tables (`cost_daily.day`, `turns_daily.day`, `tool_facts_daily.day`, `invocations_hourly.hour`), but requires rewriting `_sessions_cte` and `_scope` together, which is the highest-blast-radius change since every fact query flows through `_scope`.
+
+Technical novelty is low-to-moderate: the fix patterns (EXISTS-against-day-grain-table, left-join-for-costless-universe) are already present elsewhere in the same file for the originally-reported issue (`a80c1922c`), so the team is extending an established idiom rather than inventing one. The third gap — Copilot subagent fragments listed as separate sessions — could NOT be substantiated against this repo's current ingest/schema/query code at all; it may not be a real defect in this checkout, or may depend on CLI-side behavior outside this repo. This should be flagged back to requirements/scoping rather than estimated as backend implementation work, pending a concrete repro.
+
+Test coverage posture is good in breadth (dedicated live-Postgres suite, extensive handler-level classification tests, costless-session tests already exist) but has zero direct coverage for all three remaining gaps today — every fix will need new seam tests at both the reader (SQL-shape/live) and handler (classification) layers, and the mandatory `make test-cli-analytics-live` gate (Docker + two throwaway Postgres containers) must be run and reported. Key risk factors for complexity scoring: (1) gap (a) requires a product decision (should Overview match Sessions-list universe — this is a documented, deliberate prior choice being revisited, not a pure bug), (2) gap (d)'s fix touches the shared `_scope` helper used by every fact query in the file, raising regression surface across Overview/Cost/Users/Sessions simultaneously, (3) the file is already oversized per code-quality guidance, pushing toward an extraction as part of the fix, and (4) the ticket's own narrative (comments 1-2) describes a different, more advanced code state (MR !4659) than exists in this checkout, so scope/acceptance criteria should be re-confirmed with the reporter against this repo's actual current behavior before finalizing a plan.
